  q(ANTH,'Thin lines of dense bone seen across long bones on X-ray are called Harris lines. What do they show?','Times when growth stopped from illness or poor nutrition',['Healed fractures','Arthritis','Old fractures that healed during adulthood after a fall'],'D','Harris lines form in childhood and can give clues about periods of stress during growth.',BELL),
  q(ANTH,'A bullet hole in the skull has a bevel on the inside surface of the bone. What does this suggest?','It is an entrance wound',['It is an exit wound','It was made after death by an animal','It was caused by blunt force'],'A','The bone flakes away on the side the bullet exits, so inner beveling marks an entrance.',BELL),
  q(ANTH,'Changes in the ends of the ribs where they meet cartilage can help estimate which trait?','Age at death',['Sex only','Height','Ancestry'],'B','The sternal rib ends become more pitted and irregular as a person ages.',BELL),
  q(ENT,'Which beetles are used in labs and museums to clean flesh from skeletons?','Dermestid beetles',['Rove beetles','Carrion beetles','Ladybird beetles'],'C','Dermestid larvae eat dried tissue without damaging bone, leaving clean skeletons for study.',BELL),
  q(ENT,'Cheese skipper flies (Piophilidae) are usually found on a body at which stage?','Later decay, when tissues are drying out',['Within minutes of death','Only on living people','Only during the first hour, before blowflies arrive'],'D','Their larvae can jump by curling and snapping their bodies, which gives them their name.',BELL),
  q(ENT,'Why should investigators record temperatures at a scene where insects are collected?','Insect growth rates depend on temperature',['Insects only live in heat','It decides the cause of death','It shows who the killer is'],'A','Scene and weather station temperatures are combined to calculate how long the insects have been developing.',BELL),
  q(ENT,'Why are typical blowfly patterns less useful for bodies found in water?','Most blowflies cannot reach the body until it floats or is exposed',['Water makes flies grow faster','Water insects are always absent','Water speeds up blowfly growth so much that estimates become too short'],'B','Aquatic insects, such as caddisfly larvae, may help estimate how long a body was submerged.',BELL),
  q(ENT,'Fly larvae are found in the wounds of a living, neglected nursing home patient. What is this called?','Myiasis',['Succession','Diapause','Instar'],'C','Myiasis on a living person can be evidence of neglect and can show how long care was withheld.',BELL),
  q(BLOOD,'Blood dripping repeatedly into a pool of blood creates which pattern?','A drip pattern with many small satellite stains',['A swipe','A transfer stain','A wipe pattern made as a bloody hand moves through the pool'],'D','Drops falling into wet blood splash outward, creating small stains around the pool.',BERT),
  q(BLOOD,'Spatter of drops about 1 to 4 mm wide is often linked to which type of event?','A beating with a blunt object',['A gunshot','Blood dripping from a wound','Arterial spurting'],'A','This medium-sized impact spatter is commonly seen when a victim is struck repeatedly.',BERT),
  q(BLOOD,'The center of a drying bloodstain is wiped away, leaving only its outer rim. What is this called?','A skeletonized stain',['A void','A pool','A satellite stain'],'B','Skeletonized stains show that some time passed before the stain was disturbed.',BERT),
  q(BLOOD,'How can an alternate light source help find body fluids at a scene?','Some fluids, such as semen and saliva, fluoresce under certain wavelengths',['It makes blood glow green','It dissolves stains','It identifies the exact person who left each stain at the scene'],'C','Fluorescing areas are marked and then tested further with presumptive and confirmatory tests.',BERT),
  q(BLOOD,'Small spots that look like spatter are found near a body that lay undiscovered for weeks. What could cause them?','Flies feeding on blood and leaving fly spots',['High-velocity gunshot spatter','Cast-off from a weapon','Expirated blood coughed from the victim’s mouth and nose'],'D','Fly spots can be mistaken for spatter, but they lack a clear direction and pattern.',BERT),
  q(DNA,'Small extra peaks appearing just before true STR peaks are called:','Stutter peaks',['Pull-up peaks','Allele drop-in','Spikes from the power supply'],'A','Stutter is caused by DNA polymerase slipping during PCR and must be considered when reading profiles.',BELL),
  q(DNA,'An allele that is present in a sample does not show up in the DNA profile. What is this called?','Allele drop-out',['Stutter','A mixture','A null control'],'B','Drop-out is common with tiny or degraded samples, and analysts must be careful when comparing profiles.',BELL),
  q(DNA,'A DNA profile shows three or four alleles at several loci. What does this suggest?','A mixture of DNA from more than one person',['A single male profile','A contaminated reagent blank','An identical twin'],'C','A single person has no more than two alleles at each STR locus.',BELL),
  q(DNA,'Which technology can produce a DNA profile from a cheek swab in about two hours, outside a lab?','Rapid DNA analysis',['Capillary electrophoresis only','Mitochondrial sequencing','RFLP analysis'],'D','Rapid DNA is used for booking arrestees and in disaster victim identification.',BELL),
  q(DNA,'Why does a hair usually need its root to give a nuclear DNA profile?','The root contains cells with nuclei, while the shaft does not',['The shaft has too much DNA','The root has no cells','Nuclear DNA is found only in the hair color'],'A','Hair shafts without roots may still give mitochondrial DNA, which is less specific.',BELL),
  q(PSY,'Investigators review a person’s records and interview family to decide whether a death was suicide. What is this called?','A psychological autopsy',['A competency hearing','A polygraph test','A geographic profile'],'B','Psychological autopsies look at mood, behavior and stress before death to clarify the manner of death.',BELL),
  q(PSY,'Which FBI unit provides criminal profiling and behavioral support to investigators?','The Behavioral Analysis Unit (BAU)',['The Innocence Project','CODIS','The Automated Fingerprint Identification System'],'C','The BAU helps agencies with serial crimes, threats and unusual cases.',BELL),
  q(PSY,'What does recidivism mean?','A person who has been convicted committing another crime',['A first-time offense','A person who commits many crimes in one night','A verdict of guilty but mentally ill given to a first-time offender'],'D','Recidivism rates are used to judge how well rehabilitation and release programs work.',BELL),
  q(PSY,'An examiner who already believes a suspect is guilty sees a match that is not there. What bias is this?','Confirmation bias',['Hindsight bias','Recall bias','Survivorship bias'],'A','Labs limit exposure to case details so examiners judge the evidence on its own.',BELL),
  q(PSY,'A child repeatedly breaks rules, harms animals and destroys property. Which diagnosis may be considered?','Conduct disorder',['Antisocial personality disorder','Autism spectrum disorder','Malingering'],'B','Antisocial personality disorder is diagnosed only in adults, often after conduct disorder in childhood.',BELL)
